   <p>
    Police have launched a full-scale preliminary investigation into groups suspected of operating private revenge-for-hire services, which have recently proliferated online.

“We have been conducting intensive monitoring through the cyber analysis team as numerous advertisements for vigilante retaliation services have been posted online,” said Park Jeong-bo, head of the Seoul Metropolitan Police Agency, on Monday.

“We have identified two operations strongly suspected of involvement in crimes and are conducting a preliminary investigation before formal charges.”

Police are analyzing advertisements posted on platforms including Facebook, Google, DC Inside and Telegram to determine whether the operations are simple scams or actual criminal organizations.

Park also made clear that not only the masterminds and perpetrators, but also those who commission the crimes, would face severe punishment.

“We plan to conduct a thorough investigation on everyone involved in revenge-for-hire crimes, including those who provide information, those who carry out the acts on site and those who commission them, viewing them all as accomplices and members of criminal organizations,” Park said.

“Even making a request alone can result in a heavy sentence, and people may also end up being scammed out of money instead,” Park added, urging the public to take caution.
